  "knowledgePattern": "Find the periodic terms, then count zero partial sums",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Make a running-sum table",
      "body": "<p>Write the sequence terms for n=1,2,3,4. Underneath, keep a running total after each new term.</p>",
      "recap": "The question is about the sum so far, not just the latest term.",
      "pitfall": "A nonzero term can make the running total return to zero.",
      "pause": "Compare the pattern in the two rows.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Write initial terms before choosing a way to sum them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check why the pattern repeats",
      "body": "<p>Find how far n must increase to add a whole number of turns to both angles. Then check the sum of one complete block.</p>",
      "recap": "Repeating terms give repeating running sums only if the block total is zero.",
      "pitfall": "Count any terms left after the last complete block.",
      "pause": "Use the proved block to count the required k-values up to 100.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l17",
          "reminder": "Prove the cycle from the rule, then handle complete blocks and leftovers."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Increasing n by 3 adds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> to each trigonometric input, so the sequence repeats in blocks of three. The first three sine values are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the corresponding cosine values are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Thus the repeating terms are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Their whole-block sum is zero, and the partial sums within each block are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Among indices 1 to 99 there are 33 complete blocks, each with two zero partial sums. Index 100 is the first place of the next block, also giving zero. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>67</mn></mrow></math>, answer E.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The period follows from the formulas, not merely the first few numerical terms. Repeating terms alone would not make partial sums repeat unless the block sum were zero.</p>"
}
